== Atmosphere ==
{{Main|Atmosphere of Mars}}

[[File:Mars - Atmosphere and Cassini Crater - Hope Mission, Orbit 19 (52252929420).png|alt=Image of Mars|thumb|A broad view of Mars's atmosphere by ''Hope'' orbiter]]
Mars lost its [[magnetosphere]] 4&nbsp;billion years ago,<ref name="swind" /> possibly because of numerous asteroid strikes,<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.wired.com/2011/01/mars-dynamo-death/ |title=Multiple Asteroid Strikes May Have Killed Mars's Magnetic Field |date=20 January 2011 |access-date=30 March 2022 |magazine=Wired |last1=Grossman |first1=Lisa |archive-date=30 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131230034219/http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2011/01/mars-dynamo-death/ |url-status=live}}</ref> so the [[solar wind]] interacts directly with the Martian [[ionosphere]], lowering the atmospheric density by [[atmospheric stripping|stripping]] away atoms from the outer layer.<ref name=":7">{{Cite journal |last=Jakosky |first=Bruce M. |date=1 April 2022 |title=How did Mars lose its atmosphere and water? |journal=Physics Today |volume=75 |issue=4 |pages=62–63 |doi=10.1063/PT.3.4988 |bibcode=2022PhT....75d..62J |s2cid=247882540 |issn=0031-9228 |doi-access=free}}</ref> Both ''[[Mars Global Surveyor]]'' and ''[[Mars Express]]'' have detected ionized atmospheric particles trailing off into space behind Mars,<ref name="swind" /><ref name="swind2" /> and this atmospheric loss is being studied by the [[MAVEN]] orbiter. Compared to Earth, the [[celestial body atmosphere|atmosphere]] of Mars is quite rarefied. [[Atmospheric pressure]] on the surface today ranges from a low of {{convert|30|Pa|psi|abbr=on|lk=on}} on [[Olympus Mons]] to over {{convert|1155|Pa|psi|abbr=on}} in [[Hellas Planitia]], with a mean pressure at the surface level of {{convert|600|Pa|psi|abbr=on}}.<ref name="bolonkin09" /> The highest atmospheric density on Mars is equal to that found {{convert|35|km}}<ref name="atkinson07" /> above Earth's surface. The resulting mean surface pressure is only 0.6% of Earth's {{convert|101.3|kPa|psi|abbr=on}}. The [[scale height]] of the atmosphere is about {{convert|10.8|km}},<ref name="carr06" /> which is higher than Earth's {{convert|6|km}}, because the surface [[gravity of Mars]] is only about 38% of Earth's.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mars Facts {{!}} All About Mars |url=https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/facts |access-date=27 December 2021 |website=NASA's Mars Exploration Program |date=10 November 2017 |language=en |archive-date=10 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010080933/https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/facts/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

The atmosphere of Mars consists of about 96% [[carbon dioxide]], 1.93% [[argon]] and 1.89% [[nitrogen]] along with traces of [[oxygen]] and water.<ref name="NasaFactSheet" /><ref name="Abundance">{{cite journal |title=Abundance and Isotopic Composition of Gases in the Martian Atmosphere from the Curiosity Rover |volume=341 |issue=6143 |pages=263–266 |journal=Science |date=19 July 2013 |doi=10.1126/science.1237966 |pmid=23869014 |last1=Mahaffy |first1=P. R. |bibcode=2013Sci...341..263M |s2cid=206548973}}</ref><ref name=":7" /> The atmosphere is quite dusty, containing particulates about 1.5 [[μm]] in diameter which give the [[Extraterrestrial skies#Mars|Martian sky]] a [[tawny (color)|tawny]] color when seen from the surface.<ref name="dusty" /> It may take on a [[pink]] hue due to [[iron oxide]] particles suspended in it.<ref name="Rees2012" />

Despite repeated detections of [[methane on Mars]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shekhtman |first=Lonnie |date=22 April 2024 |title=Why is Methane Seeping on Mars? NASA Scientists Have New Ideas – NASA Science |url=https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mars/why-is-methane-seeping-on-mars-nasa-scientists-have-new-ideas/ |access-date=1 January 2026 |language=en-US}}</ref> there is no scientific consensus as to its origin.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sutter |first=Paul |date=16 November 2024 |title=What's Behind the Martian Methane Mystery? |url=https://www.universetoday.com/articles/whats-behind-the-martian-methane-mystery |access-date=1 January 2026 |website=Universe Today |language=en}}</ref> One suggestion is that methane exists on Mars and that its concentration fluctuates seasonally.<ref name="Sample2018">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/jun/07/nasa-mars-rover-finds-organic-matter-in-ancient-lake-bed |title=Nasa Mars rover finds organic matter in ancient lake bed |work=The Guardian |first=Ian |last=Sample |date=7 June 2018 |access-date=12 June 2018 |archive-date=18 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618030502/https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/jun/07/nasa-mars-rover-finds-organic-matter-in-ancient-lake-bed |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="plumes" /><ref name="nature460" /> The existence of methane could be produced by non-biological process such as [[serpentinite|serpentinization]] involving water, carbon dioxide, and the mineral [[olivine]], which is known to be common on Mars, or by Martian life.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Chang |first=Kenneth |date=1 April 2019 |title=Something on Mars Is Producing Gas Usually Made by Living Things on Earth |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/01/science/mars-methane-gas.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401190931/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/01/science/mars-methane-gas.html |archive-date=1 April 2019 |access-date=1 January 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en}}</ref><ref name="olivine" />[[File:PIA18613-MarsMAVEN-Atmosphere-3UV-Views-20141014.jpg|thumb|upright=1.8|[[Atmosphere of Mars|Escaping atmosphere]] on Mars ([[carbon]], [[oxygen]], and [[hydrogen]]) by [[MAVEN]] in [[Ultraviolet–visible spectroscopy|UV]]<ref name="NASA-20141014-NJ">{{cite web |last1=Jones |first1=Nancy |last2=Steigerwald |first2=Bill |last3=Brown |first3=Dwayne |last4=Webster |first4=Guy |date=14 October 2014 |title=NASA Mission Provides Its First Look at Martian Upper Atmosphere |url=http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/news.php?release=2014-351 |access-date=15 October 2014 |publisher=NASA |archive-date=19 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141019184946/http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/news.php?release=2014-351 |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>|300x300px]]

Compared to Earth, its higher concentration of atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> and lower surface pressure may be why sound is attenuated more on Mars, where natural sources are rare apart from the wind. Using acoustic recordings collected by the ''Perseverance'' rover, researchers concluded that the speed of sound there is approximately 240&nbsp;m/s for frequencies below 240&nbsp;Hz, and 250&nbsp;m/s for those above.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Wright |first=Katherine |date=22 March 2022 |title=Sound Speed Measured on Mars |url=https://physics.aps.org/articles/v15/43 |journal=[[Physics (magazine)|Physics]] |volume=15 |article-number=43 |doi=10.1103/Physics.15.43 |bibcode=2022PhyOJ..15...43W |s2cid=247720720 |doi-access=free |access-date=6 April 2022 |archive-date=12 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220412151942/https://physics.aps.org/articles/v15/43 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Maurice |first1=S. |last2=Chide |first2=B. |last3=Murdoch |first3=N. |last4=Lorenz |first4=R. D. |last5=Mimoun |first5=D. |last6=Wiens |first6=R. C. |last7=Stott |first7=A. |last8=Jacob |first8=X. |last9=Bertrand |first9=T. |last10=Montmessin |first10=F. |last11=Lanza |first11=N. L. |date=1 April 2022 |title=In situ recording of Mars soundscape |journal=Nature |volume=605 |issue=7911 |pages=653–658 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04679-0 |pmid=35364602 |pmc=9132769 |bibcode=2022Natur.605..653M |s2cid=247865804 |issn=0028-0836}}</ref>

[[Aurora on Mars|Aurora]]s have been detected on Mars.<ref>{{cite web |title=In an ultraviolet glow, auroras on Mars spotted by UAE orbiter |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/science/space/ultraviolet-glow-auroras-mars-spotted-uae-orbiter-rcna1356 |first=Denise |last=Chow |access-date=7 December 2021 |date=7 December 2021 |website=NBC News |language=en |archive-date=7 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207221852/https://www.nbcnews.com/science/space/ultraviolet-glow-auroras-mars-spotted-uae-orbiter-rcna1356 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Auroras on Mars – NASA Science |url=https://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2015/11may_aurorasonmars/ |website=science.nasa.gov |date=10 May 2015 |access-date=12 May 2015 |archive-date=14 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150514060513/http://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2015/11may_aurorasonmars/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref name="NASA-20150318">{{cite web |last1=Brown |first1=Dwayne |last2=Neal-Jones |first2=Nancy |last3=Steigerwald |first3=Bill |last4=Scott |first4=Jim |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press/2015/march/nasa-spacecraft-detects-aurora-and-mysterious-dust-cloud-around-mars |title=NASA Spacecraft Detects Aurora and Mysterious Dust Cloud around Mars |publisher=NASA |id=Release 15-045 |date=18 March 2015 |access-date=18 March 2015 |archive-date=19 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150319152358/http://www.nasa.gov/press/2015/march/nasa-spacecraft-detects-aurora-and-mysterious-dust-cloud-around-mars/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref> Because Mars lacks a global magnetic field, the types and distribution of auroras there differ from those on Earth;<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Deighan |first1=J. |last2=Jain |first2=S. K. |last3=Chaffin |first3=M. S. |last4=Fang |first4=X. |last5=Halekas |first5=J. S. |last6=Clarke |first6=J. T. |last7=Schneider |first7=N. M. |last8=Stewart |first8=A. I. F. |last9=Chaufray |first9=J.-Y. |last10=Evans |first10=J. S. |last11=Stevens |first11=M. H. |date=October 2018 |title=Discovery of a proton aurora at Mars |url=http://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0538-5 |journal=Nature Astronomy |volume=2 |issue=10 |pages=802–807 |doi=10.1038/s41550-018-0538-5 |bibcode=2018NatAs...2..802D |s2cid=105560692 |issn=2397-3366 |access-date=5 April 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522145256/https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0538-5 |url-status=live}}</ref> rather than being mostly restricted to polar regions as is the case on Earth, a Martian aurora can encompass the planet.<ref name=":8">{{Cite journal |last1=Schneider |first1=N. M. |last2=Jain |first2=S. K. |last3=Deighan |first3=J. |last4=Nasr |first4=C. R. |last5=Brain |first5=D. A. |last6=Larson |first6=D. |last7=Lillis |first7=R. |last8=Rahmati |first8=Ali |last9=Halekas |first9=J. S. |last10=Lee |first10=C. O. |last11=Chaffin |first11=M. S. |date=16 August 2018 |title=Global Aurora on Mars During the September 2017 Space Weather Event |journal=Geophysical Research Letters |volume=45 |issue=15 |pages=7391–7398 |doi=10.1029/2018GL077772 |bibcode=2018GeoRL..45.7391S |s2cid=115149852 |doi-access=free |hdl=10150/631256 |hdl-access=free}}</ref> In September 2017, NASA reported [[radiation]] levels on the surface of the planet Mars were temporarily [[Orders of magnitude (radiation)|doubled]], and were associated with an [[aurora]] 25 times brighter than any observed earlier, due to a massive, and unexpected, [[Coronal mass ejection|solar storm]] in the middle of the month.<ref name=":8" /><ref name="nasa20170929">{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/large-solar-storm-sparks-global-aurora-and-doubles-radiation-levels-on-the-martian-surface |title=Large Solar Storm Sparks Global Aurora and Doubles Radiation Levels on the Martian Surface |publisher=NASA |first1=Guy |last1=Webster |first2=Nancy |last2=Neal-Jones |first3=Jim |last3=Scott |first4=Deb |last4=Schmid |first5=Laurie |last5=Cantillo |first6=Dwayne |last6=Brown |date=29 September 2017 |access-date=9 January 2018 |archive-date=1 October 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171001181055/https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/large-solar-storm-sparks-global-aurora-and-doubles-radiation-levels-on-the-martian-surface/ |url-status=dead}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

=== Climate ===
{{Main|Climate of Mars}}

[[File:Mars Before and During Global Dust Storm.jpg|thumb|Mars without (on left) and with a global dust storm in July 2001 (on right), including different visible water ice cloud covers, as seen by the Hubble Space Telescope]]
[[File:PIA22546-Mars-AnnualCO2ice-N&SPoles-20180806.gif|thumb|Rendering of the change of [[dry ice|CO<sub>2</sub> ice]] (not water ice) coverage on the north (left) and south (right) poles of Mars between northern and southern summer.]]
Mars has seasons, alternating between its northern and southern hemispheres, similar to on Earth. Additionally the orbit of Mars has, compared to Earth's, a large [[Orbital eccentricity|eccentricity]] and approaches [[Apsis|perihelion]] when it is summer in its southern hemisphere and winter in its northern, and aphelion when it is winter in its southern hemisphere and summer in its northern. As a result, the seasons in its southern hemisphere are more extreme and the seasons in its northern are milder than would otherwise be the case. The summer temperatures in the south can be warmer than the equivalent summer temperatures in the north by up to {{convert|30|C-change}}.<ref name="goodman97" />

Martian surface temperatures vary from lows of about {{convert|-110|C|F}} to highs of up to {{convert|35|C|F}} in equatorial summer.<ref name="hot" /> The wide range in temperatures is due to the thin atmosphere which cannot store much solar heat, the low atmospheric pressure (about 1% that of the [[atmosphere of Earth]]), and the low [[thermal inertia]] of Martian soil.<ref name="nasa_surface" /> The planet is 1.52 times as far from the Sun as Earth, resulting in just 43% of the amount of sunlight.<ref name="disc920901" /><ref>{{cite web |last=Hille |first=Karl |date=18 September 2015 |title=The Fact and Fiction of Martian Dust Storms |url=http://www.nasa.gov/feature/goddard/the-fact-and-fiction-of-martian-dust-storms |access-date=25 December 2021 |website=NASA |archive-date=2 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160302231423/http://www.nasa.gov/feature/goddard/the-fact-and-fiction-of-martian-dust-storms/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Mars has the largest [[dust storm]]s in the Solar System, reaching speeds of over {{convert|100|mph|km/h|order=flip|abbr=on}}. These can vary from a storm over a small area, to gigantic storms that cover the entire planet. They tend to occur when Mars is closest to the Sun, and have been shown to increase global temperature.<ref name="philips01" /> Seasons also produce [[dry ice]] covering [[Martian polar ice caps|polar ice caps]].<ref name="a652">{{cite web | title=Dry Ice on Mars | website=NASA Science | date=6 April 2011 | url=https://science.nasa.gov/resource/dry-ice-on-mars/ | access-date=1 May 2025}}</ref>

== Hydrology ==
{{Main|Water on Mars}}

{{See also|Groundwater on Mars}}
[[File:PIA26046-Mars-BuriedIceMaps-20231026.jpg|thumb|upright=1.8|Mars contains water, though mostly as dust covered polar layers of ice, as mapped in this image.]]

While Mars contains [[Water on terrestrial planets of the Solar System|water in larger amounts]], most of it is dust covered water ice at the [[Martian polar ice caps]].<ref name="specials1" /><ref name="bbc040124" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Holt |first1=John W. |last2=Safaeinili |first2=Ali |last3=Plaut |first3=Jeffrey J. |last4=Head |first4=James W. |last5=Phillips |first5=Roger J. |last6=Seu |first6=Roberto |last7=Kempf |first7=Scott D. |last8=Choudhary |first8=Prateek |last9=Young |first9=Duncan A. |last10=Putzig |first10=Nathaniel E. |last11=Biccari |first11=Daniela |date=21 November 2008 |title=Radar Sounding Evidence for Buried Glaciers in the Southern Mid-Latitudes of Mars |url=https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1164246 |journal=Science |volume=322 |issue=5905 |pages=1235–1238 |bibcode=2008Sci...322.1235H |doi=10.1126/science.1164246 |issn=0036-8075 |jstor=20145331 |pmid=19023078 |s2cid=36614186 |hdl=11573/67950 |access-date=22 April 2022 |archive-date=22 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162701/https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1164246 |url-status=live|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name="sci299" /><ref name=":3">{{cite web |title=Polar Caps |url=https://marsed.asu.edu/mep/ice/polar-caps |access-date=6 January 2022 |website=Mars Education at Arizona State University |archive-date=28 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210528133135/https://marsed.asu.edu/mep/ice/polar-caps |url-status=live}}</ref>
The volume of water ice in the south polar ice cap, if melted, would be enough to cover most of the surface of the planet with a depth of {{convert|11|m}}.<ref name="nasa070315" />

Water in its liquid form cannot persist on the surface due to Mars's low atmospheric pressure, which is less than 1% that of Earth.<ref name="nasa.gov">{{cite web |title=NASA – NASA Rover Finds Clues to Changes in Mars' Atmosphere |url=http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/msl/news/msl20121102.html |publisher=NASA |access-date=19 October 2014 |archive-date=26 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226060801/https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/msl/news/msl20121102.html%20 |url-status=dead}} {{PD-notice}}</ref> Only at the lowest of elevations are the pressure and temperature high enough for liquid water to exist for short periods.<ref name="mars-in-depth" /><ref name="jgr110" />

Although little water is present in the atmosphere, there is enough to produce clouds of water ice and different cases of [[snow]] and frost, often mixed with snow of [[carbon dioxide]] [[dry ice]].<ref name="k104">{{cite web | title=Carbon-Dioxide Snowfall on Mars | website=NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) | date=11 September 2012 | url=https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/images/pia16138-carbon-dioxide-snowfall-on-mars/ | access-date=19 April 2025}}</ref>

===Past hydrosphere===
[[File:Artist’s impression of Mars four billion years ago.jpg|thumb|Artist's impression of Mars four billion years ago]]
[[Geomorphology|Landforms]] visible on Mars strongly suggest that liquid water has existed on the planet's surface. Huge linear swathes of scoured ground, known as [[outflow channels]], cut across the surface in about 25 places. These are thought to be a record of erosion caused by the catastrophic release of water from subsurface aquifers, though some of these structures have been hypothesized to result from the action of glaciers or lava.<ref name="Kerr2005" /><ref name="Jaeger2007" /> One of the larger examples, [[Ma'adim Vallis]], is {{convert|700|km}} long, much greater than the Grand Canyon, with a width of {{convert|20|km}} and a depth of {{convert|2|km}} in places. It is thought to have been carved by flowing water early in Mars's history.<ref name="lucchita_rosanova" /> The youngest of these channels is thought to have formed only a few million years ago.<ref name="nature434" />

Elsewhere, particularly on the oldest areas of the Martian surface, finer-scale, dendritic [[Valley networks (Mars)|networks of valleys]] are spread across significant proportions of the landscape. Features of these valleys and their distribution strongly imply that they were carved by [[surface runoff|runoff]] resulting from precipitation in early Mars history. Subsurface water flow and [[groundwater sapping]] may play important subsidiary roles in some networks, but precipitation was probably the root cause of the incision in almost all cases.<ref name="CraddockHoward2002" />

Along craters and canyon walls, there are thousands of features that appear similar to terrestrial [[gullies]]. The gullies tend to be in the highlands of the Southern Hemisphere and face the Equator; all are poleward of 30° latitude. A number of authors have suggested that their formation process involves liquid water, probably from melting ice,<ref name="sci288" /><ref name="nasa061206" /> although others have argued for formation mechanisms involving carbon dioxide frost or the movement of dry dust.<ref name="bbc061206" /><ref name="nasa061206b" /> No partially degraded gullies have formed by weathering and no superimposed impact craters have been observed, indicating that these are young features, possibly still active.<ref name="nasa061206" /> Other geological features, such as [[River delta|deltas]] and [[alluvial fan]]s preserved in craters, are further evidence for warmer, wetter conditions at an interval or intervals in earlier Mars history.<ref name="Lewis2006" /> Such conditions necessarily require the widespread presence of [[Volcanic crater lake|crater lakes]] across a large proportion of the surface, for which there is independent mineralogical, sedimentological and geomorphological evidence.<ref name="Matsubara2011" /> Further evidence that liquid water once existed on the surface of Mars comes from the detection of specific minerals such as [[hematite]] and [[goethite]], both of which sometimes form in the presence of water.<ref name="nasa040303" />

=== History of observations and findings of water evidence ===
{{Main|Water on Mars|Chronology of discoveries of water on Mars}}

[[File:Perspective view of Korolev crater.jpg|thumb|As top surface layer water appears readily visible at some places on Mars, as in this polar [[Korolev (Martian crater)|crater called Korolev]], seen here in [[3D projection]].]]
The chemical signature of water vapor on Mars was first unequivocally demonstrated in 1963 by spectroscopy using an Earth-based telescope.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Jakosky |first=Bruce M. |last2=Mellon |first2=Michael T. |date=1 April 2004 |title=Special Issue: Water on Mars |url=https://physicstoday.aip.org/features/special-issue-water-on-mars |journal=Physics Today |language=en |volume=57 |issue=4 |pages=71–76 |doi=10.1063/1.1752425 |issn=0031-9228|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | url=https://adsabs.harvard.edu/full/1963ApJ...137.1319S | bibcode=1963ApJ...137.1319S | title=Letter to the Editor: The Detection of Water Vapor on Mars | last1=Spinrad | first1=Hyron | last2=Münch | first2=Guido | last3=Kaplan | first3=Lewis D. | journal=The Astrophysical Journal | date=1963 | volume=137 | page=1319 | doi=10.1086/147613 }}</ref> In 2004, ''Opportunity'' detected the mineral [[jarosite]]. This forms only in the presence of acidic water, showing that water once existed on Mars.<ref name="nasa101001" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Elwood Madden |first1=M. E. |last2=Bodnar |first2=R. J. |last3=Rimstidt |first3=J. D. |date=October 2004 |title=Jarosite as an indicator of water-limited chemical weathering on Mars |url=http://www.nature.com/articles/nature02971 |journal=Nature |volume=431 |issue=7010 |pages=821–823 |doi=10.1038/nature02971 |issn=0028-0836 |pmid=15483605 |s2cid=10965423 |access-date=22 April 2022 |archive-date=3 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220303070003/https://www.nature.com/articles/nature02971 |url-status=live|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The ''Spirit'' rover found concentrated deposits of [[silica]] in 2007 that indicated wet conditions in the past, and in December 2011, the mineral [[gypsum]], which also forms in the presence of water, was found on the surface by NASA's Mars rover Opportunity.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 December 2007 |title=Mars Rover Investigates Signs of Steamy Martian Past |url=https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/mars-rover-investigates-signs-of-steamy-martian-past |access-date=5 April 2022 |website=NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) |archive-date=6 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706160839/https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/mars-rover-investigates-signs-of-steamy-martian-past |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="nasa" /><ref name="nationalgeographic" /> It is estimated that the amount of water in the upper mantle of Mars, represented by [[hydroxyl ion]]s contained within Martian minerals, is equal to or greater than that of Earth at 50–300 parts per million of water, which is enough to cover the entire planet to a depth of {{convert|200|-|1000|m}}.<ref name="nationalgeographic1" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=McCubbin |first1=Francis M. |last2=Hauri |first2=Erik H. |last3=Elardo |first3=Stephen M. |last4=Vander Kaaden |first4=Kathleen E. |last5=Wang |first5=Jianhua |last6=Shearer |first6=Charles K. |date=August 2012 |title=Hydrous melting of the martian mantle produced both depleted and enriched shergottites |url=http://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/geology/article/40/8/683/130947/Hydrous-melting-of-the-martian-mantle-produced |journal=Geology |volume=40 |issue=8 |pages=683–686 |bibcode=2012Geo....40..683M |doi=10.1130/G33242.1 |issn=1943-2682|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

On 18 March 2013, [[NASA]] reported evidence from instruments on the [[Curiosity (rover)|''Curiosity'' rover]] of [[mineral hydration]], likely hydrated [[calcium sulfate]], in several [[Rock (geology)|rock samples]] including the broken fragments of [[Tintina (rock)|"Tintina" rock]] and [[List of rocks on Mars#Curiosity|"Sutton Inlier" rock]] as well as in [[vein (geology)|veins]] and [[nodule (geology)|nodules]] in other rocks like "Knorr" rock and "Wernicke" rock.<ref name="NASA-20130318">{{cite web |last1=Webster |first1=Guy |last2=Brown |first2=Dwayne |date=18 March 2013 |title=Curiosity Mars Rover Sees Trend in Water Presence |url=http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl/news/whatsnew/index.cfm?FuseAction=ShowNews&NewsID=1446 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130424111259/http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl/news/whatsnew/index.cfm?FuseAction=ShowNews&NewsID=1446 |archive-date=24 April 2013 |access-date=20 March 2013 |publisher=NASA}} {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref name="BBC-20130319">{{cite news |last=Rincon |first=Paul |date=19 March 2013 |title=Curiosity breaks rock to reveal dazzling white interior |work=BBC News |publisher=BBC |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/science-environment-21340279 |access-date=19 March 2013 |archive-date=8 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308051408/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/science-environment-21340279 |url-status=live}}</ref> Analysis using the rover's [[Curiosity (rover)#Dynamic Albedo of Neutrons (DAN)|DAN instrument]] provided evidence of subsurface water, amounting to as much as 4% water content, down to a depth of {{convert|60|cm}}, during the rover's traverse from the ''[[Bradbury Landing]]'' site to the ''Yellowknife Bay'' area in the ''[[Glenelg, Mars|Glenelg]]'' terrain.<ref name="NASA-20130318" /> In September 2015, NASA announced that they had found strong evidence of hydrated [[brine]] flows in [[recurring slope lineae]], based on spectrometer readings of the darkened areas of slopes.<ref>{{cite web |date=28 September 2015 |title=NASA Confirms Evidence That Liquid Water Flows on Today's Mars |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasa-confirms-evidence-that-liquid-water-flows-on-today-s-mars |access-date=28 September 2015 |publisher=NASA |archive-date=28 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150928161524/http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasa-confirms-evidence-that-liquid-water-flows-on-today-s-mars/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Drake |first=Nadia |date=28 September 2015 |title=NASA Finds 'Definitive' Liquid Water on Mars |url=http://news.nationalgeographic.com/2015/09/150928-mars-liquid-water-confirmed-surface-streaks-space-astronomy/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150930194303/http://news.nationalgeographic.com/2015/09/150928-mars-liquid-water-confirmed-surface-streaks-space-astronomy/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=30 September 2015 |access-date=29 September 2015 |website=National Geographic News}}</ref><ref name="Ojhaetal2015">{{cite journal |last1=Ojha |first1=L. |last2=Wilhelm |first2=M. B. |last3=Murchie |first3=S. L. |last4=McEwen |first4=A. S. |last5=Wray |first5=J. J. |last6=Hanley |first6=J. |last7=Massé |first7=M. |last8=Chojnacki |first8=M. |year=2015 |title=Spectral evidence for hydrated salts in recurring slope lineae on Mars |journal=Nature Geoscience |volume=8 |issue=11 |pages=829–832 |bibcode=2015NatGe...8..829O |doi=10.1038/ngeo2546 |s2cid=59152931}}</ref> These streaks flow downhill in Martian summer, when the temperature is above −23&nbsp;°C, and freeze at lower temperatures.<ref>{{cite web |last=Moskowitz |first=Clara |title=Water Flows on Mars Today, NASA Announces |url=http://www.scientificamerican.com/article/water-flows-on-mars-today-nasa-announces/ |access-date=29 September 2015 |website=[[Scientific American]] |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515071020/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/water-flows-on-mars-today-nasa-announces/ |url-status=live}}</ref> These observations supported earlier hypotheses, based on timing of formation and their rate of growth, that these dark streaks resulted from water flowing just below the surface.<ref name="SeasonalFlows">{{cite journal |last1=McEwen |first1=Alfred |last2=Lujendra |first2=Ojha |last3=Dundas |first3=Colin |last4=Mattson |first4=Sarah |last5=Bryne |first5=S |last6=Wray |first6=J |last7=Cull |first7=Selby |last8=Murchie |first8=Scott |last9=Thomas |first9=Nicholas |last10=Gulick |first10=Virginia |date=5 August 2011 |title=Seasonal Flows on Warm Martian Slopes |url=https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1204816 |journal=Science |volume=333 |issue=6043 |pages=740–743 |bibcode=2011Sci...333..740M |doi=10.1126/science.1204816 |pmid=21817049 |s2cid=10460581 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150929112931/https://sciencescape.org/paper/21817049 |archive-date=29 September 2015 |access-date=28 September 2015|url-access=subscription }}</ref> However, later work suggested that the lineae may be dry, granular flows instead, with at most a limited role for water in initiating the process.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dundas |first1=Colin M. |last2=McEwen |first2=Alfred S. |last3=Chojnacki |first3=Matthew |last4=Milazzo |first4=Moses P. |last5=Byrne |first5=Shane |last6=McElwaine |first6=Jim N. |last7=Urso |first7=Anna |date=December 2017 |title=Granular flows at recurring slope lineae on Mars indicate a limited role for liquid water |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-017-0012-5 |journal=Nature Geoscience |volume=10 |issue=12 |pages=903–907 |bibcode=2017NatGe..10..903D |doi=10.1038/s41561-017-0012-5 |issn=1752-0908 |s2cid=24606098 |hdl-access=free |hdl=10150/627918 |access-date=3 April 2022 |archive-date=22 November 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171122143221/https://www.nature.com/articles/s41561-017-0012-5 |url-status=live}}</ref> A definitive conclusion about the presence, extent, and role of liquid water on the Martian surface remains elusive.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Schorghofer |first=Norbert |date=12 February 2020 |title=Mars: Quantitative Evaluation of Crocus Melting behind Boulders |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=890 |issue=1 |pages=49 |bibcode=2020ApJ...890...49S |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/ab612f |issn=1538-4357 |s2cid=213701664 |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Wray |first=James J. |date=30 May 2021 |title=Contemporary Liquid Water on Mars? |url=https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-earth-072420-071823 |journal=Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences |volume=49 |issue=1 |pages=141–171 |bibcode=2021AREPS..49..141W |doi=10.1146/annurev-earth-072420-071823 |issn=0084-6597 |s2cid=229425641 |access-date=3 April 2022 |archive-date=3 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210503080828/https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-earth-072420-071823 |url-status=live|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Researchers suspect much of the low northern plains of the planet were [[Mars ocean hypothesis|covered with an ocean]] hundreds of meters deep, though this theory remains controversial.<ref name="Head1999" /> In March 2015, scientists stated that such an ocean might have been the size of Earth's [[Arctic Ocean]]. This finding was derived from the ratio of [[Protium (isotope)|protium]] to [[deuterium]] in the modern Martian atmosphere compared to that ratio on Earth. The amount of Martian deuterium (D/H = 9.3 ± 1.7 10<sup>−4</sup>) is five to seven times the amount on Earth (D/H = 1.56 10<sup>−4</sup>), suggesting that ancient Mars had significantly higher levels of water. Results from the ''Curiosity'' rover had previously found a high ratio of deuterium in [[Gale Crater]], though not significantly high enough to suggest the former presence of an ocean. Other scientists caution that these results have not been confirmed, and point out that Martian climate models have not yet shown that the planet was warm enough in the past to support bodies of liquid water.<ref name="NYT-20150305">{{cite news |last=Kaufman |first=Marc |date=5 March 2015 |title=Mars Had an Ocean, Scientists Say, Pointing to New Data |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/06/science/mars-had-an-ocean-scientists-say-pointing-to-new-data.html |access-date=5 March 2015 |archive-date=7 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200307000937/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/03/06/science/mars-had-an-ocean-scientists-say-pointing-to-new-data.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Near the northern polar cap is the {{convert|81.4|km}} wide [[Korolev (Martian crater)|Korolev Crater]], which the [[Mars Express]] orbiter found to be filled with approximately {{convert|2200|km3}} of water ice.<ref name="TG1218">{{cite news |last1=Sample |first1=Ian |date=21 December 2018 |title=Mars Express beams back images of ice-filled Korolev crater |newspaper=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/dec/21/mars-express-beams-back-images-of-ice-filled-korolev-crater |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-date=8 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200208045902/https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/dec/21/mars-express-beams-back-images-of-ice-filled-korolev-crater |url-status=live}}</ref>

In November 2016, NASA reported finding a large amount of underground ice in the [[Utopia Planitia]] region. The volume of water detected has been estimated to be equivalent to the volume of water in [[Lake Superior]] (which is 12,100 cubic kilometers<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 October 2010 |title=EPA; Great Lakes; Physical Facts |url=http://www.epa.gov/glnpo/physfacts.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101029215637/http://www.epa.gov/glnpo/physfacts.html |archive-date=29 October 2010 |access-date=15 February 2023}}</ref>).<ref>{{cite web |date=22 November 2016 |title=Mars Ice Deposit Holds as Much Water as Lake Superior |url=http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/news.php?release=2016-299 |access-date=23 November 2016 |publisher=NASA |archive-date=26 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226060807/https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref name="NASA-20161122">{{cite web |author=Staff |date=22 November 2016 |title=Scalloped Terrain Led to Finding of Buried Ice on Mars |url=http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21136 |access-date=23 November 2016 |publisher=NASA |archive-date=26 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226060804/https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA21136%20 |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref> During observations from 2018 through 2021, the ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter spotted indications of water, probably subsurface ice, in the Valles Marineris canyon system.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mitrofanov |first1=I. |last2=Malakhov |first2=A. |last3=Djachkova |first3=M. |last4=Golovin |first4=D. |last5=Litvak |first5=M. |last6=Mokrousov |first6=M. |last7=Sanin |first7=A. |last8=Svedhem |first8=H. |last9=Zelenyi |first9=L. |date=March 2022 |title=The evidence for unusually high hydrogen abundances in the central part of Valles Marineris on Mars |journal=Icarus |volume=374 |article-number=114805 |bibcode=2022Icar..37414805M |doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2021.114805 |s2cid=244449654 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

== Orbital motion ==
{{Main|Orbit of Mars}}

{{See also|Timekeeping on Mars}}{{Anchor|Viewing from Earth|Orbit and rotation}}[[File:Solar system orrery inner planets.gif|alt=Mars circling the Sun further and slower than Earth|thumb|upright=1.2|Orbit of Mars and other Inner Solar System planets]]
Mars's average distance from the Sun is roughly {{convert|143|e6mi|e6km|abbr=unit|order=flip}}, and its orbital period is 687 (Earth) days. The solar day (or [[Mars sol|sol]]) on Mars is only slightly longer than an Earth day: 24 hours, 39 minutes, and 35.244 seconds.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Badescu |first1=Viorel |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BnPE37Ms5awC&pg=PA600 |title=Mars: Prospective Energy and Material Resources |date=2009 |publisher=Springer Science & Business Media |isbn=978-3-642-03629-3 |edition=illustrated |page=600 |access-date=20 May 2016 |archive-date=4 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230304004803/https://books.google.com/books?id=BnPE37Ms5awC&pg=PA600 |url-status=live}}</ref> A Martian year is equal to 1.8809 Earth years, or 1 year, 320 days, and 18.2 hours.<ref name="NasaFactSheet" /> The [[gravitational potential]] difference and thus the [[Delta-v budget|delta-v needed]] to transfer between Mars and Earth is the second lowest for Earth.<ref name="Petropoulos Longuski Bonfiglio 2000 pp. 776–783">{{cite journal |last1=Petropoulos |first1=Anastassios E. |last2=Longuski |first2=James M. |last3=Bonfiglio |first3=Eugene P. |year=2000 |title=Trajectories to Jupiter via Gravity Assists from Venus, Earth, and Mars |journal=Journal of Spacecraft and Rockets |publisher=American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics (AIAA) |volume=37 |issue=6 |pages=776–783 |bibcode=2000JSpRo..37..776P |doi=10.2514/2.3650 |issn=0022-4650}}</ref><ref name="Taylor 2020">{{cite web |last=Taylor |first=Chris |date=9 July 2020 |title=Welcome to Cloud City: The case for going to Venus, not Mars |url=https://mashable.com/feature/venus-mars-space-exploration |access-date=21 October 2022 |website=Mashable |archive-date=21 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221021222623/https://mashable.com/feature/venus-mars-space-exploration |url-status=live}}</ref>

The axial tilt of Mars is 25.19° relative to its [[orbital plane]], which is similar to the [[Ecliptic#Obliquity of the ecliptic|axial tilt of Earth]].<ref name="NasaFactSheet" /> As a result, Mars has seasons like Earth, though on Mars they are nearly twice as long because its orbital period is that much longer. In the present day, the orientation of the [[north pole]] of Mars is close to the star [[Deneb]].<ref name="barlow08" />

Mars has a relatively pronounced [[orbital eccentricity]] of about 0.09; of the seven other planets in the Solar System, only [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]] has a larger orbital eccentricity. It is known that in the past, Mars has had a much more circular orbit. At one point, 1.35&nbsp;million Earth years ago, Mars had an eccentricity of roughly 0.002, much less than that of Earth today.<ref name="mars_eccentricity" /> Mars's [[Milankovitch cycles|cycle of eccentricity]] is 96,000 Earth years compared to Earth's cycle of 100,000 years.<ref name="Meeus2003" />

Mars has its closest approach to Earth ([[opposition (astronomy)|opposition]]) in a [[synodic period]] of 779.94 days. It should not be confused with Mars [[conjunction (astronomy)|conjunction]], where the Earth and Mars are at opposite sides of the Solar System and form a straight line crossing the Sun. The average time between the successive oppositions of Mars, its synodic period, is 780 days; but the number of days between successive oppositions can range from 764 to 812.<ref name="Meeus2003" /> The distance at close approach varies between about {{convert|54|and|103|e6km|e6mi|abbr=unit}} due to the planets' [[ellipse|elliptical]] orbits, which causes comparable variation in [[angular size]].<ref name="Laskar2003" /> At their furthest Mars and Earth can be as far as {{convert|401|e6km|e6mi|abbr=unit}} apart.<ref name="m455">{{cite web |last1=Tillman |first1=Nola Taylor |last2=Dobrijevic |first2=Daisy |title=How Long Does It Take to Get to Mars? |website=Space.com |date=20 January 2022 |url=https://www.space.com/24701-how-long-does-it-take-to-get-to-mars.html#:~:text=The%20two%20planets%20are%20farthest,miles%20(225%20million%20km). |access-date=23 August 2024}}</ref> Mars comes into opposition from Earth every 2.1 years. The planets come into opposition near Mars's [[perihelion]] in 2003, 2018 and 2035, with the 2020 and 2033 events being particularly close to perihelic opposition.<ref name=":1">{{cite web |title=Mars Opposition |url=https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/night-sky/opposition |access-date=7 December 2021 |website=NASA's Mars Exploration Program |date=10 November 2017 |archive-date=5 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231005222312/https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/night-sky/opposition/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=5 October 2021 |title=Why is Mars sometimes bright and sometimes faint? |url=https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/why-is-mars-sometimes-bright-and-sometimes-faint/ |access-date=7 December 2021 |website=EarthSky |archive-date=7 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207122120/https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/why-is-mars-sometimes-bright-and-sometimes-faint/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 November 2005 |title=Close encounter: Mars at opposition |url=https://www.spacetelescope.org/images/opo0534l/ |access-date=1 April 2022 |website=ESA/Hubble |archive-date=10 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910011439/http://www.spacetelescope.org/images/opo0534l/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Mars - 2020 Opposition (crop).jpg|thumb|upright=0.8|Mars seen through a 16-inch amateur telescope, at 2020 opposition]]

The mean [[apparent magnitude]] of Mars is +0.71 with a standard deviation of 1.05.<ref name="Mallama_and_Hilton" /> Because the orbit of Mars is eccentric, the magnitude at [[opposition (planets)|opposition]] from the Sun can range from about −3.0 to −1.4.<ref name="MallamaSky" /> The minimum brightness is magnitude +1.86 when the planet is near [[Apsis|aphelion]] and in [[conjunction (astronomy)|conjunction]] with the Sun.<ref name="Mallama_and_Hilton" /> At its brightest, Mars (along with [[Jupiter]]) is second only to Venus in apparent brightness.<ref name="Mallama_and_Hilton" /> Mars usually appears distinctly yellow, orange, or red. When farthest away from Earth, it is more than seven times farther away than when it is closest. Mars is usually close enough for particularly good viewing once or twice at 15-year or 17-year intervals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mars Close Approach |url=https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/night-sky/close-approach |access-date=18 January 2022 |website=NASA's Mars Exploration Program |date=10 November 2017 |archive-date=8 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191108232313/https://mars.nasa.gov/all-about-mars/night-sky/close-approach/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Optical ground-based telescopes are typically limited to resolving features about {{convert|300|km}} across when Earth and Mars are closest because of Earth's atmosphere.<ref name="usra">{{cite web |title=Slide 2 Earth Telescope View of Mars |url=http://www.lpi.usra.edu/publications/slidesets/redplanet2/slide_2.html |work=The Red Planet: A Survey of Mars |publisher=Lunar and Planetary Institute |access-date=28 November 2011 |archive-date=18 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210518203239/https://www.lpi.usra.edu/publications/slidesets/redplanet2/slide_2.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

As Mars approaches opposition, it begins a period of [[Apparent retrograde motion|retrograde motion]], which means it will appear to move backwards in a looping curve with respect to the background stars. This retrograde motion lasts for about 72 days, and Mars reaches its peak apparent brightness in the middle of this interval.<ref name="zeilik02" />

== Moons ==
{{Main|Moons of Mars|Phobos (moon)|Deimos (moon)}}

[[File:PIA17351-ApparentSizes-MarsDeimosPhobos-EarthMoon.jpg|thumb|The moons of Mars [[Deimos (moon)|Deimos]] and [[Phobos (moon)|Phobos]] as seen from Mars, compared at [[angular size|apparent size]] to the in apparent and actual size much larger [[Moon]] as seen from Earth. If they would be as far away from Mars as the Moon from Earth, they would appear as faint star-like features in the Martian sky.]]
[[File:Phobos fly-by animation ESA223006.gif|thumb|Orbits of natural and artificial satellites around Mars at scale, with the furthest (Deimos) at {{convert|23,460|km|mi|abbr=on}}]]

Mars has two relatively small (compared to Earth's) natural moons, [[Phobos (moon)|Phobos]] (about {{convert|22|km|abbr=on}} in diameter) and [[Deimos (moon)|Deimos]] (about {{convert|12|km|abbr=on}} in diameter), which orbit at {{convert|9376|km|mi|abbr=on}} and {{convert|23,460|km|mi|abbr=on}} around the planet. The origin of both moons is unclear, although a popular theory states that they were asteroids captured into Martian orbit.<ref name="esa31031" />

Both satellites were discovered in 1877 by [[Asaph Hall]] and were named after the characters [[Phobos (mythology)|Phobos]] (the deity of panic and fear) and [[Deimos (mythology)|Deimos]] (the deity of terror and dread), twins from [[Greek mythology]] who accompanied their father [[Ares]], god of war, into battle.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Planetary Names |url=https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Page/Planets#MartianSystem |access-date=30 May 2022 |website=planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov |archive-date=30 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231230164721/https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Page/Planets#MartianSystem |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Mars (mythology)|Mars]] was the Roman equivalent to Ares. In modern [[Greek language|Greek]], the planet retains its ancient name ''Ares'' (Aris: ''Άρης'').<ref name=":6" />

From the surface of Mars, the motions of Phobos and Deimos appear different from that of the Earth's satellite, the [[Moon]]. Phobos rises in the west, sets in the east, and rises again in just 11 hours. Deimos, being only just outside [[synchronous orbit]]{{Snd}}where the orbital period would match the planet's period of rotation{{Snd}}rises as expected in the east, but slowly. Because the orbit of Phobos is below a synchronous altitude, [[tidal force]]s from Mars are gradually lowering its orbit. In about 50&nbsp;million years, it could either crash into Mars's surface or break up into a ring structure around the planet.<ref>{{cite web |title=Phobos |url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/moons/mars-moons/phobos/in-depth |access-date=12 January 2022 |date=19 December 2019 |website=NASA Solar System Exploration |archive-date=12 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220112004526/https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/moons/mars-moons/phobos/in-depth/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

The origin of the two satellites is not well understood. Their low albedo and [[carbonaceous chondrite]] composition have been regarded as similar to asteroids, supporting a capture theory. The unstable orbit of Phobos would seem to point toward a relatively recent capture. But both have [[circular orbit]]s near the equator, which is unusual for captured objects, and the required capture dynamics are complex. Accretion early in the history of Mars is plausible, but would not account for a composition resembling asteroids rather than Mars itself, if that is confirmed.<ref>{{cite web |title=Explaining the Birth of the Martian Moons |date=23 September 2016 |url=https://aasnova.org/2016/09/23/explaining-the-birth-of-the-martian-moons/ |access-date=13 December 2021 |language=en-US |website=AAS Nova |publisher=[[American Astronomical Society]] |archive-date=13 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211213022350/https://aasnova.org/2016/09/23/explaining-the-birth-of-the-martian-moons/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Mars may have yet-undiscovered moons, smaller than {{convert|50|to|100|m}} in diameter, and a dust ring is predicted to exist between Phobos and Deimos.<ref name="adler">{{cite conference |last1=Adler |first1=M. |last2=Owen |first2=W. |last3=Riedel |first3=J. |date=June 2012 |title=Use of MRO Optical Navigation Camera to Prepare for Mars Sample Return |url=http://www.lpi.usra.edu/meetings/marsconcepts2012/pdf/4337.pdf |conference=Concepts and Approaches for Mars Exploration. 12–14 June 2012. Houston, Texas. |issue=1679 |at=4337 |bibcode=2012LPICo1679.4337A |access-date=28 August 2012 |archive-date=26 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226060810/https://www.lpi.usra.edu/meetings/marsconcepts2012/pdf/4337.pdf%20 |url-status=live}}</ref>

A third possibility for their origin as satellites of Mars is the involvement of a third body or a type of impact disruption. More-recent lines of evidence for Phobos having a highly porous interior,<ref name="Andert" /> and suggesting a composition containing mainly [[phyllosilicates]] and other minerals known from Mars,<ref name="Giuranna" /> point toward an origin of Phobos from material ejected by an impact on Mars that reaccreted in Martian orbit, similar to the [[Giant impact hypothesis|prevailing theory]] for the origin of Earth's satellite. Although the [[visible and near-infrared]] (VNIR) spectra of the moons of Mars resemble those of outer-belt asteroids, the [[thermal infrared]] spectra of Phobos are reported to be inconsistent with [[chondrite]]s of any class.<ref name="Giuranna" /> It is also possible that Phobos and Deimos were fragments of an older moon, formed by debris from a large impact on Mars, and then destroyed by a more recent impact upon the satellite.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Dynamical evidence for Phobos and Deimos as remnants of a disrupted common progenitor |doi=10.1038/s41550-021-01306-2 |journal=Nature Astronomy |date=22 February 2021 |volume=5 |pages=539–543 |first1=Amirhossein |last1=Bagheri |first2=Amir |last2=Khan |first3=Michael |last3=Efroimsky |first4=Mikhail |last4=Kruglyakov |first5=Domenico |last5=Giardini |issue=6 |bibcode=2021NatAs...5..539B |s2cid=233924981}}</ref>

More recently, a study conducted by a team of researchers from multiple countries suggests that a lost moon, at least fifteen times the size of Phobos, may have existed in the past. By analyzing rocks which point to tidal processes on the planet, it is possible that these tides may have been regulated by a past moon.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Benningfield |first=Damond |date=11 December 2025 |title=Ancient Lake on Mars May Have Risen and Fallen Under the Tidal Pull of a Long Lost Moon |url=https://www.zmescience.com/space/ancient-lake-on-mars-may-have-risen-and-fallen-under-the-tidal-pull-of-a-long-lost-moon/ |access-date=13 December 2025 |website=ZME Science |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Human observations and exploration ==
{{Main|Exploration of Mars|History of Mars observation}}

The history of observations of Mars is marked by oppositions of Mars when the planet is closest to Earth and hence is most easily visible, which occur every couple of years. Even more notable are the [[perihelic opposition]]s of Mars, which are distinguished because Mars is close to perihelion, making it even closer to Earth.<ref name=":1" />

=== Ancient observations ===
The ancient [[Sumer]]ians named Mars [[Nergal]], the god of war and plague. During Sumerian times, Nergal was a minor deity of little significance, but, during later times, his main cult center was the city of [[Nineveh]].<ref name="Rabkin2005">{{cite book |last1=Rabkin |first1=Eric S. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=a2QP30zybNkC&pg=PA11 |title=Mars: A Tour of the Human Imagination |date=2005 |publisher=Praeger |isbn=978-0-275-98719-0 |location=Westport, Connecticut |pages=9–11}}</ref> In Mesopotamian texts, Mars is referred to as the "star of judgement of the fate of the dead".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Thompson |first1=Henry O. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=kc0UAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA125 |title=Mekal: The God of Beth-Shan |date=1970 |publisher=E. J. Brill |location=Leiden |page=125}}</ref> The existence of Mars as a wandering object in the night sky was also recorded by the ancient [[Egyptian astronomy|Egyptian astronomers]] and, by 1534 BCE, they were familiar with the [[Apparent retrograde motion|retrograde motion]] of the planet.<ref name="paob85" /> By the period of the [[Neo-Babylonian Empire]], the [[Babylonian astronomy|Babylonian astronomers]] were making regular records of the positions of the planets and systematic observations of their behavior. For Mars, they knew that the planet made 37 [[Orbital period|synodic periods]], or 42 circuits of the zodiac, every 79 years. They invented arithmetic methods for making minor corrections to the predicted positions of the planets.<ref name="north08" /><ref name="swerdlow98" /> In [[Ancient Greece]], the planet was known as {{langx|grc|[[Pyroeis|Πυρόεις]]|label=none}}.<ref>{{cite book |last=Cicero |first=Marcus Tullius |url=https://archive.org/details/marcitulliicicer00cice |title=De Natura Deorum |publisher=Methuen |year=1896 |location=London |translator=Francis Brooks |trans-title=On the Nature of the Gods}}</ref> Commonly, the Greek name for the planet now referred to as Mars, was Ares. It was the Romans who named the planet Mars, for their god of war, often represented by the sword and shield of the planet's namesake.<ref>{{Cite web |last=NASA |date=9 October 2022 |title=All About Mars |url=https://mars.nasa.gov/allaboutmars/mystique/history/early/ |website=mars.nasa.gov |access-date=10 October 2022 |archive-date=10 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221010032729/https://mars.nasa.gov/allaboutmars/mystique/history/early/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

In the fourth century BCE, [[Aristotle]] noted that Mars disappeared behind the Moon during an [[occultation]], indicating that the planet was farther away.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Stephenson |first=F. Richard |date=November 2000 |title=A Lunar Occultation of Mars Observed by Aristotle |journal=Journal for the History of Astronomy |volume=31 |issue=4 |pages=342–344 |bibcode=2000JHA....31..342S |doi=10.1177/002182860003100405 |issn=0021-8286 |s2cid=125518456 }}</ref> [[Ptolemy]], a Greek living in [[Alexandria]],<ref name="harland" /> attempted to address the problem of the orbital motion of Mars. Ptolemy's model and his collective work on astronomy was presented in the multi-volume collection later called the ''[[Almagest]]'' (from the Arabic for "greatest"), which became the authoritative treatise on [[Medieval astronomy|Western astronomy]] for the next fourteen centuries.<ref>{{citation |last=McCluskey |first=S. C. |title=Astronomies and Cultures in Early Medieval Europe |pages=20–21 |year=1998 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-77852-7}}</ref> Literature from ancient China confirms that Mars was known by [[Chinese astronomy|Chinese astronomers]] by no later than the fourth century BCE.<ref name="needham_ronan85" /> In the [[East Asian]] cultures, Mars is traditionally referred to as the "fire star" ({{lang|zh|火星}}) based on the [[Wuxing (Chinese philosophy)|''Wuxing'' system]].<ref>{{cite book |last=de Groot |first=Jan Jakob Maria |title=Religion in China – Universism: A Key to the Study of Taoism and Confucianism |date=1912 |publisher=G. P. Putnam's Sons |series=American Lectures on the History of Religions, volume 10 |page=300 |chapter=Fung Shui |oclc=491180 |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZAaP7dyjCrAC&pg=PA300 |access-date=5 January 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Crump |first=Thomas |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mk3vxD3Ib_sC&pg=PT43 |title=The Japanese Numbers Game: The Use and Understanding of Numbers in Modern Japan |date=1992 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-0-415-05609-0 |series=Nissan Institute/Routledge Japanese Studies Series |pages=39–40}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Hulbert |first=Homer Bezaleel |url=https://archive.org/details/passingkorea01hulbgoog |title=The Passing of Korea |date=1909 |publisher=Doubleday, Page & Company |page=[https://archive.org/details/passingkorea01hulbgoog/page/n538 426] |oclc=26986808 |orig-year=1906}}</ref>

=== Early modern observations ===
[[File:Kepler Mars retrograde.jpg|thumb|upright=.9|Diagram of the [[geocentric]] trajectory of Mars through several periods of [[apparent retrograde motion]] in [[Johannes Kepler]]'s ''Astronomia Nova'' (1609)]]

In 1609 [[Johannes Kepler]] published a 10-year study of Martian orbit,<ref name="v529">{{cite web | last=Cooper | first=Keith | title=Johannes Kepler: Unlocking the Secrets of Planetary Motion | website=Space | date=22 December 2023 | url=https://www.space.com/15787-johannes-kepler.html | access-date=16 April 2025}}</ref> using the [[diurnal parallax]] of Mars, measured by [[Tycho Brahe]], to make a preliminary calculation of the relative distance to the planet.<ref name="taton03" /> From Brahe's observations of Mars, Kepler deduced that the planet orbited the Sun not in a circle, but in an [[ellipse]]. Moreover, Kepler showed that Mars sped up as it approached the Sun and slowed down as it moved farther away, in a manner that later physicists would explain as a consequence of the [[conservation of angular momentum]].<ref name="TMU">{{Cite book |last1=Frautschi |first1=Steven C. |title=The Mechanical Universe: Mechanics and Heat |title-link=The Mechanical Universe |last2=Olenick |first2=Richard P. |last3=Apostol |first3=Tom M. |last4=Goodstein |first4=David L. |date=2007 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-71590-4 |edition=Advanced |location=Cambridge [Cambridgeshire] |oclc=227002144 |author-link=Steven Frautschi |author-link3=Tom M. Apostol |author-link4=David L. Goodstein}}</ref>{{Rp|pages=433–437}}

[[File:Lowell - Mars (1894) - Huyghens' drawing of the Syrtis Major, Nov. 28, 1659.jpg|thumb|First drawn record of Martian features (Manuscript K), by [[Christiaan Huygens]], observed on 28 November 1659.<ref name="v462">{{cite web | last=Huygens | first=Christiaan | title=I. (Manuscrit K). 1657-1659., Oeuvres complètes. Tome XV. Observations astronomiques, Christiaan Huygens | publisher=DBNL | url=https://www.dbnl.org/tekst/huyg003oeuv15_01/huyg003oeuv15_01_0002.php | language=nl | access-date=15 April 2025}}</ref> The feature is probably [[Syrtis Major Planum]]. Huygens calculated from the moving of the features the rotation of Mars.]]

In 1610 the first use of a telescope for astronomical observation, including Mars, was performed by Italian astronomer [[Galileo Galilei]].<ref name="jha15" /> With the telescope the diurnal parallax of Mars was again measured in an effort to determine the Sun-Earth distance. This was first performed by [[Giovanni Domenico Cassini]] in 1672. The early parallax measurements were hampered by the quality of the instruments.<ref name="hirschfeld01" /> The only [[occultation]] of Mars by Venus observed was that of 13 October 1590, seen by [[Michael Maestlin]] at [[Heidelberg]].<ref name="sat57" />

=== Martian "canals" ===

{{Main|Martian canals}}

[[File:Mars_-_MEC-1_Prototype._LOC_2013593160.jpg|thumb|upright=1.5|A 1962 map of Mars published by the U.S. [[Aeronautical Chart and Information Center]], showing canals snaking through the Martian landscape. At the time, the existence of canals was still highly controversial as no close-up pictures of Mars had been taken (until [[Mariner 4]]'s flyby in 1965).]]
By the 19th century, the resolution of telescopes reached a level sufficient for surface features to be identified. On 5 September 1877, a perihelic opposition to Mars occurred. The Italian astronomer [[Giovanni Schiaparelli]] used a {{convert|22|cm|adj=on}} telescope in [[Milan]] to help produce the first detailed map of Mars. These maps notably contained features he called ''canali'', which, with the possible exception of the natural canyon [[Valles Marineris]], were later shown to be an [[optical illusion]]. These ''canali'' were supposedly long, straight lines on the surface of Mars, to which he gave names of famous rivers on Earth. His term, which means "channels" or "grooves", was popularly mistranslated in English as "canals".<ref>{{cite web |last=Milner |first=Richard |date=6 October 2011 |title=Tracing the Canals of Mars: An Astronomer's Obsession |url=https://www.space.com/13197-mars-canals-water-history-lowell.html |access-date=25 December 2021 |website=Space.com |archive-date=25 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211225043755/https://www.space.com/13197-mars-canals-water-history-lowell.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="sagan80" />

Influenced by the observations, the orientalist [[Percival Lowell]] founded [[Lowell Observatory|an observatory]] which had 30- and 45-centimetre (12- and 18-in) telescopes. The observatory was used for the exploration of Mars during the last good opportunity in 1894, and the following less favorable oppositions. He published several books on Mars and life on the planet, which had a great influence on the public.<ref name="basalla06" /><ref name="NYT-20151001">{{cite news |last=Dunlap |first=David W. |date=1 October 2015 |title=Life on Mars? You Read It Here First. |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/30/insider/life-on-mars-you-read-it-here-first.html |access-date=1 October 2015 |archive-date=2 October 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151002192143/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/30/insider/life-on-mars-you-read-it-here-first.html |url-status=live}}</ref> The ''canali'' were independently observed by other astronomers, such as [[Henri Joseph Perrotin]] and [[Louis Thollon]] in Nice, using one of the largest telescopes of that time.<ref name="maria_lane05" /><ref name="ba3" />

The seasonal changes (consisting of the diminishing of the polar caps and the dark areas formed during Martian summers) in combination with the canals led to speculation about life on Mars, and it was a long-held belief that Mars contained vast seas and vegetation. As bigger telescopes were used, fewer long, straight ''canali'' were observed. During observations in 1909 by [[Antoniadi]] with an {{convert|84|cm|adj=on}} telescope, irregular patterns were observed, but no ''canali'' were seen.<ref name="zahnle01" />

=== First exploration ===
[[File:M04 12.jpg|thumb|The very first two images from, and of, another planet (Mars), by [[Mariner 4]],<ref name="u445">{{cite web | title=Attempt to Take Pictures of Black Space Scrubbed | website=NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) | date=21 August 1965 | url=https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/attempt-to-take-pictures-of-black-space-scrubbed/ | access-date=15 April 2025}}</ref> the second spacecraft to reach Mars and the first to transmit data (15 July 1965). The data disproved Mars being hospitable and having canals.]]

The first spacecraft from Earth to visit Mars was [[Mars 1]] of the [[Soviet space program|Soviet Union]], which flew by in 1963, but contact was lost en route. [[NASA]]'s ''[[Mariner 4]]'' followed and became the first spacecraft to successfully transmit from Mars; launched on 28 November 1964, it made its closest approach to the planet on 15 July 1965. ''Mariner 4'' detected the weak Martian radiation belt, measured at about 0.1% that of Earth, and captured the first images of another planet from deep space.<ref>{{cite web |date=20 December 2017 |title=Mariner 04 |url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/mariner-04/in-depth |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200803192306/https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/mariner-04/in-depth/ |archive-date=3 August 2020 |access-date=9 February 2020 |website=NASA Solar System Exploration |quote=The Mariner 4 mission, the second of two Mars flyby attempts launched in 1964 by NASA, was one of the great early successes of the agency, and indeed the Space Age, returning the very first photos of another planet from deep space.}} {{PD-notice}} {{cite web |title=Spacecraft – Details – Mariner 4 |url=https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1964-077A |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180904174225/https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraftDisplay.do?id=1964-077A |archive-date=4 September 2018 |access-date=9 February 2020 |website=NASA – NSSDCA |quote=Mariner 4...represented the first successful flyby of the planet Mars, returning the first pictures of the martian surface. These represented the first images of another planet ever returned from deep space.}} {{PD-notice}}</ref>

Once [[spacecraft]] visited the planet during the 1960s and 1970s, many previous concepts of Mars were radically broken. After the results of the ''Viking'' life-detection experiments, the hypothesis of a dead planet was generally accepted.<ref name="ward_brownlee00" /> The data from ''Mariner 9'' and ''Viking'' allowed better maps of Mars to be made.

===Renewed exploration===
Until 1997 and after Viking 1 shut down in 1982, Mars was only visited by three unsuccessful probes, two flying past without contact ([[Phobos 1]], 1988; [[Mars Observer]], 1993), and one ([[Phobos 2]] 1989) malfunctioning in orbit before reaching its destination [[Phobos (moon)|Phobos]].

In 1997 [[Mars Pathfinder]] became the first successful rover mission beyond the Moon and started together with ''[[Mars Global Surveyor]]'' (operated until late 2006) an uninterrupted active robotic presence at Mars that has lasted until today. It produced complete, extremely detailed maps of the Martian topography, magnetic field and surface minerals.<ref name="Distant worlds: milestones in planetary exploration" />

Starting with these missions a range of new improved crewless [[spacecraft]], including [[orbiter]]s, [[Mars lander|landers]], and [[Mars rover|rovers]], have been sent to Mars, with successful missions by the NASA (United States), [[Jaxa]] ([[Japan]]), [[ESA]], [[United Kingdom]], [[ISRO]] ([[India]]), [[Roscosmos]] (Russia), the [[Mohammed bin Rashid Space Centre|United Arab Emirates]], and [[CNSA]] ([[China]]) to study the planet's surface, climate, and geology,<ref>{{cite web |date=29 July 2020 |title=Why we explore Mars—and what decades of missions have revealed |url=https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/mars-exploration-article |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210218212120/https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/mars-exploration-article |url-status=dead |archive-date=18 February 2021 |access-date=7 December 2021 |website=National Geographic |first=Nadia |last=Drake |author-link=Nadia Drake |language=en}}</ref> uncovering the different elements of the history and dynamic of the hydrosphere of Mars and possible traces of ancient life.

[[File:Mars 2020 selfie containing both perseverance rover and ingenuity.gif|thumb|[[Self-portrait]] of ''[[Perseverance (rover)|Perseverance]]'' rover and ''[[Ingenuity (helicopter)|Ingenuity]]'' helicopter (left) at [[Wright Brothers Field]], 2021]]

===Current missions===
{{As of|2023}}, Mars is host to ten functioning [[spacecraft]].

Eight are in orbit'': [[2001 Mars Odyssey]]'', ''[[Mars Express]]'', ''[[Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter]]'', ''[[MAVEN]]'', ''[[ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter]]'', the ''[[Emirates Mars Mission|Hope]]'' orbiter, and the ''[[Tianwen-1]]'' orbiter.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Strickland |first=Ashley |date=12 February 2021 |title=Meet the orbiters that help rovers on Mars talk to Earth |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/02/12/world/nasa-maven-mro-mars-orbiters-scn/index.html |access-date=22 March 2022 |website=CNN |archive-date=22 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322041827/https://www.cnn.com/2021/02/12/world/nasa-maven-mro-mars-orbiters-scn/index.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hill |first=Tanya |title=As new probes reach Mars, here's what we know so far from trips to the red planet |url=http://theconversation.com/as-new-probes-reach-mars-heres-what-we-know-so-far-from-trips-to-the-red-planet-153791 |access-date=22 March 2022 |work=The Conversation |date=9 February 2021 |archive-date=16 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220216192112/https://theconversation.com/as-new-probes-reach-mars-heres-what-we-know-so-far-from-trips-to-the-red-planet-153791 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Another two are on the surface: the [[Mars Science Laboratory]] ''[[Curiosity (rover)|Curiosity]]'' rover and the ''[[Perseverance (rover)|Perseverance]]'' rover.<ref name="nyt-china-2021">{{cite news |last1=Myers |first1=Steven Lee |last2=Chang |first2=Kenneth |title=China's Mars Rover Mission Lands on the Red Planet |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/14/science/china-mars.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20211228/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/14/science/china-mars.html |archive-date=28 December 2021 |url-access=limited |access-date=15 May 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=14 May 2021}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

Collected maps are available online at websites including [[Google Mars]]. NASA provides two online tools: Mars Trek, which provides visualizations of the planet using data from 50 years of exploration, and [[Experience Curiosity]], which simulates traveling on Mars in 3-D with ''Curiosity''.<ref>{{cite web |title=New Online Tools Bring NASA's Journey to Mars to a New Generation |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/new-online-tools-bring-nasa-s-journey-to-mars-to-new-generation-of-explorers |access-date=5 August 2015 |date=5 August 2015 |website=NASA |archive-date=7 August 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150807234318/http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/new-online-tools-bring-nasa-s-journey-to-mars-to-new-generation-of-explorers/ |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.wired.co.uk/article/nasa-mars-trek |title=Explore the Red Planet with Nasa's Mars Trek |magazine=Wired UK |date=10 July 2015 |access-date=31 March 2022 |first=Daniel |last=Culpan |archive-date=31 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220331183510/https://www.wired.co.uk/article/nasa-mars-trek |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Future===
Planned missions to Mars include:

* NASA's [[EscaPADE]] spacecraft, launched on 13 November 2025.<ref name="nasa2024062024">{{Cite tweet |number=1827079568470995106 |user=blueorigin |title=#NewGlenn's inaugural mission aims to send @NASA's ESCAPADE to Mars, with a launch date no earlier than October 13.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.blueorigin.com/missions/ng-2 |title=New Glenn Mission NG-2 |publisher=Blue Origin |access-date=14 November 2025 |archive-date=13 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113025923/https://www.blueorigin.com/missions/ng-2 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite tweet |number= 1989074636055998558 |user=blueorigin |title= LIFTOFF! New Glenn clears the tower at LC-36, carrying @NASA's ESCAPADE mission to Mars. |date=13 November 2025 |access-date=13 November 2025}}</ref>
* The [[Rosalind Franklin (rover)|''Rosalind Franklin'' rover]] mission, designed to search for evidence of past life, which was intended to be launched in 2018 but has been repeatedly delayed, with a launch date pushed to 2028 at the earliest.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 May 2016 |title=Second ExoMars mission moves to next launch opportunity in 2020 |url=https://www.esa.int/Newsroom/Press_Releases/Second_ExoMars_mission_moves_to_next_launch_opportunity_in_2020 |access-date=24 March 2022 |website=European Space Agency |archive-date=9 February 2026 |archive-url=http://web.archive.org/web/20260209090212/https://www.esa.int/Newsroom/Press_Releases/Second_ExoMars_mission_moves_to_next_launch_opportunity_in_2020 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=12 March 2020 |title=ExoMars to take off for the Red Planet in 2022 |url=https://www.esa.int/Newsroom/Press_Releases/ExoMars_to_take_off_for_the_Red_Planet_in_2022 |access-date=24 March 2022 |website=European Space Agency |archive-date=19 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319062237/https://www.esa.int/Newsroom/Press_Releases/ExoMars_to_take_off_for_the_Red_Planet_in_2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Amos |first=Jonathan |date=17 March 2022 |title=Joint Europe-Russia Mars rover project is parked |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-60782932 |access-date=24 March 2022 |archive-date=6 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406171653/https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-60782932 |url-status=live}}</ref> The project was restarted in 2024 with additional funding.<ref>{{Cite web |title=A saga for Rosalind Franklin – To Mars and back |url=https://blogs.esa.int/to-mars-and-back/2024/05/09/a-saga-for-rosalind-franklin/ |access-date=13 May 2024 |language=en-US}}</ref>
* A current concept for a joint [[NASA]]-[[European Space Agency|ESA]] mission to [[NASA-ESA Mars Sample Return|return samples from Mars]] would launch in 2026.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.planetary.org/articles/nasa-esa-latest-msr-plan |title=NASA, ESA Officials Outline Latest Mars Sample Return Plans |website=planetary.org |access-date=9 September 2019 |date=13 August 2019 |archive-date=4 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804091735/https://www.planetary.org/articles/nasa-esa-latest-msr-plan |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Mars Sample Return Campaign |url=https://mars.nasa.gov/msr/ |access-date=31 January 2022 |website=mars.nasa.gov |language=en |archive-date=15 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220615020149/https://mars.nasa.gov/msr/ |url-status=live}}</ref>
* China's [[Tianwen-3]], a sample return mission, scheduled to launch in either 2028 or 2030.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://spacenews.com/china-to-launch-mars-sample-return-mission-in-2028-will-follow-planetary-protection-guidelines/ |title=China to launch Mars sample return mission in 2028, will follow planetary protection guidelines/ |access-date=30 September 2025 |website=Spacenews |date=5 September 2024 |language=en | vauthors = Jones A }}</ref>

{{As of|2024|February}}, debris from these types of missions has reached over seven tons. Most of it consists of crashed and inactive spacecraft as well as discarded components.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kilicpublished |first=Cagri |date=28 September 2022 |title=Mars is littered with 15,694 pounds of human trash from 50 years of robotic exploration |url=https://www.space.com/mars-littered-with-human-trash |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=Space.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Humans have already dumped 7 tonnes of junk on Mars, map reveals |url=https://www.wionews.com/science/map-shows-human-made-debris-piling-up-on-mars-over-seven-tonnes-of-space-junk-lying-around-red-planet-686201 |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=WION |date=2 February 2024 |language=en-us}}</ref>

In April 2024, NASA selected several companies to begin studies on providing commercial services to further enable robotic science on Mars. Key areas include establishing telecommunications, payload delivery and surface imaging.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NASA Selects Commercial Service Studies to Enable Mars Robotic Science |url=https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-selects-commercial-service-studies-to-enable-mars-robotic-science |access-date=1 May 2024 |website=NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 2025, an ad hoc committee was convened by the [[National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine]] (NASEM) to prioritize science objectives to guide human exploration on Mars.<ref name=":2">{{Cite Q|https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q139854992}}</ref><ref>{{Cite Q|https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q137730561}}</ref> Out of hundreds of possible projects, the four panels of the committee (Astrobiology, Geosciences, Atmospheric Science and Space Physics, and Biological and Physical Sciences and Human Factors) highlighted eleven as the top science objectives to pursue, with the top priority being searching for current or extinct indigenous life on Mars.<ref name=":2" /> 

== Habitability and habitation ==
{{Main|1 = Life on Mars}}

[[File:41586 2025 9413 Fig3 HTML.png|thumb|upright=1.2|Layering, nodules, reaction fronts and organic detections marked in an image from ancient fossilized mud at [[Cheyava Falls]], being the most promising finds potentially showing traces of remains of ancient Martian life.]]

During the late 19th century, it was widely accepted in the astronomical community that Mars had life-supporting qualities, including the presence of oxygen and water.<ref name="CampbellWW" /> However, in 1894 [[William Wallace Campbell|W. W. Campbell]] at [[Lick Observatory]] observed the planet and found that "if water vapor or oxygen occur in the atmosphere of Mars it is in quantities too small to have been detected by spectroscopes then available".<ref name="CampbellWW" /> That observation contradicted many of the measurements of the time and was not widely accepted.<ref name="CampbellWW" /> Campbell and [[Vesto Slipher|V. M. Slipher]] repeated the study in 1909 using better instruments, but with the same results. It was not until the findings were confirmed by [[Walter Sydney Adams|W. S. Adams]] in 1925 that the myth of the Earth-like habitability of Mars was finally broken.<ref name="CampbellWW" /> However, even in the 1960s, articles were published on Martian biology, putting aside explanations other than life for the seasonal changes on Mars.<ref name="science136_3510" />

The current understanding of [[planetary habitability]]{{Snd}}the ability of a world to develop environmental conditions favorable to the emergence of life{{Snd}}favors planets that have liquid water on their surface. Most often this requires the orbit of a planet to lie within the [[circumstellar habitable zone|habitable zone]], which for the Sun is estimated to extend from within the orbit of Earth to about that of Mars.<ref name="Kopparapu2013b">{{cite journal |last1=Kopparapu |first1=Ravi Kumar |last2=Ramirez |first2=Ramses |last3=Kasting |first3=James F. |last4=Eymet |first4=Vincent |last5=Robinson |first5=Tyler D. |last6=Mahadevan |first6=Suvrath |last7=Terrien |first7=Ryan C. |last8=Domagal-Goldman |first8=Shawn |last9=Meadows |first9=Victoria |last10=Deshpande |first10=Rohit |date=2013 |title=Habitable Zones Around Main-Sequence Stars: New Estimates |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=765 |issue=2 |pages=131 |arxiv=1301.6674 |bibcode=2013ApJ...765..131K |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/765/2/131 |s2cid=76651902}}</ref> During perihelion, Mars dips inside this region, but Mars's thin (low-pressure) atmosphere prevents liquid water from existing over large regions for extended periods. The past flow of liquid water demonstrates the planet's potential for habitability. Recent evidence has suggested that any water on the Martian surface may have been too salty and acidic to support regular terrestrial life.<ref name="saltlife" />

The environmental conditions on Mars are a challenge to sustaining organic life: the planet has little [[heat transfer]] across its surface, it has poor insulation against [[Solar wind#Atmospheres|bombardment]] by the [[solar wind]] due to the absence of a magnetosphere and has insufficient atmospheric pressure to retain water in a liquid form (water instead [[Sublimation (phase transition)|sublimes]] to a gaseous state). Mars is nearly, or perhaps totally, geologically dead; the end of volcanic activity has apparently stopped the recycling of chemicals and minerals between the surface and interior of the planet.<ref name="hannsson97" />

Evidence suggests that the planet was once significantly more habitable than it is today, but whether living [[organism]]s ever existed there remains unknown. The [[Viking probes|''Viking'' probes]] of the mid-1970s carried experiments designed to detect microorganisms in Martian soil at their respective landing sites and had positive results, including a temporary increase in {{chem2|CO2}} production on exposure to water and nutrients. This sign of life was later disputed by scientists, resulting in a continuing debate, with NASA scientist [[Gilbert Levin]] asserting that ''Viking'' may have found life.<ref name="nytobit">{{cite web |last=Chang |first=Kenneth |date=4 August 2021 |title=Gilbert V. Levin, Who Said He Found Signs of Life on Mars, Dies at 97 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/science/space/gilbert-v-levin-dead.html |access-date=4 August 2021 |work=The New York Times |archive-date=4 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210804185707/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/science/space/gilbert-v-levin-dead.html |url-status=live}}</ref> A 2014 analysis of Martian meteorite EETA79001 found [[chlorate]], [[perchlorate]], and [[nitrate]] ions in sufficiently high concentrations to suggest that they are widespread on Mars. UV and X-ray radiation would turn chlorate and perchlorate ions into other, highly [[redox|reactive]] [[Oxyanion|oxychlorines]], indicating that any organic molecules would have to be buried under the surface to survive.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kounaves |first1=S. P. |year=2014 |title=Evidence of martian perchlorate, chlorate, and nitrate in Mars meteorite EETA79001: implications for oxidants and organics |journal=Icarus |volume=229 |pages=206–213 |bibcode=2014Icar..229..206K |doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2013.11.012}}</ref>

Small quantities of [[methane]] and [[formaldehyde]] detected by Mars orbiters are both claimed to be possible evidence for life, as these [[chemical compound]]s would quickly break down in the Martian atmosphere.<ref name="icarus172" /><ref name="form" /> Alternatively, these compounds may instead be replenished by volcanic or other geological means, such as [[serpentinite]].<ref name="olivine" /> [[Impactite|Impact glass]], formed by the impact of meteors, which on Earth can preserve signs of life, has also been found on the surface of the impact craters on Mars.<ref name="brown20140418">{{cite news |last=Nickel |first=Mark |date=18 April 2014 |title=Impact glass stores biodata for millions of years |publisher=Brown University |url=https://news.brown.edu/articles/2014/04/impactglass |access-date=9 June 2015 |archive-date=17 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150617122630/https://news.brown.edu/articles/2014/04/impactglass |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Schultz2014">{{cite journal |last1=Schultz |first1=P. H. |last2=Harris |first2=R. Scott |last3=Clemett |first3=S. J. |last4=Thomas-Keprta |first4=K. L. |last5=Zárate |first5=M. |date=June 2014 |title=Preserved flora and organics in impact melt breccias |journal=[[Geology (journal)|Geology]] |volume=42 |issue=6 |pages=515–518 |bibcode=2014Geo....42..515S |doi=10.1130/G35343.1 |s2cid=39019154 |hdl-access=free |hdl=2060/20140013110}}</ref> Likewise, the glass in impact craters on Mars could have preserved signs of life, if life existed at the site.<ref name="nasapr20150608">{{cite press release |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasa-spacecraft-detects-impact-glass-on-surface-of-mars |title=NASA Spacecraft Detects Impact Glass on Surface of Mars |publisher=NASA |first1=Dwayne |last1=Brown |first2=Guy |last2=Webster |first3=Kevin |last3=Stacey |date=8 June 2015 |access-date=9 June 2015 |archive-date=9 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150609132637/http://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasa-spacecraft-detects-impact-glass-on-surface-of-mars/ |url-status=live}} {{PD-notice}}</ref><ref name="brown20150608">{{cite news |last=Stacey |first=Kevin |date=8 June 2015 |title=Martian glass: Window into possible past life? |publisher=Brown University |url=https://news.brown.edu/articles/2015/06/marsglass |access-date=9 June 2015 |archive-date=11 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150611003248/http://news.brown.edu/articles/2015/06/marsglass |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="sciam20150612">{{cite news |last=Temming |first=Maria |date=12 June 2015 |title=Exotic Glass Could Help Unravel Mysteries of Mars |work=Scientific American |url=http://www.scientificamerican.com/article/exotic-glass-could-help-unravel-mysteries-of-mars/ |access-date=15 June 2015 |archive-date=15 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150615010829/http://www.scientificamerican.com/article/exotic-glass-could-help-unravel-mysteries-of-mars/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[Cheyava Falls]] rock discovered on Mars in June 2024 has been designated by NASA as a "potential [[biosignature]]" and was core sampled by the [[Perseverance (rover)|Perseverance]] rover for possible return to Earth and further examination. Although highly intriguing, no definitive final determination on a biological or abiotic origin of this rock can be made with the data currently available.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NASA Says Mars Rover Discovered Potential Biosignature Last Year - NASA |url=https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-says-mars-rover-discovered-potential-biosignature-last-year/ |access-date=10 September 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Human mission proposals ===
[[File:NextSTEP_mars-soil-excavation.jpg|thumb|upright=1.2|A NASA [[ISRU]] system concept for autonomous robotic excavation and processing of Mars soil to extract water for use in exploration missions.]]

Several plans for a [[human mission to Mars]] have been proposed, but none have come to fruition. The NASA Authorization Act of 2017 directed NASA to study the feasibility of a crewed Mars mission in the early 2030s; the resulting report concluded that this would be unfeasible.<ref>{{cite web |date=21 March 2017 |title=S.442 – National Aeronautics and Space Administration Transition Authorization Act of 2017 |url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/115th-congress/senate-bill/442/text |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330021740/https://www.congress.gov/bill/115th-congress/senate-bill/442/text |archive-date=30 March 2022 |access-date=29 March 2022 |website=congress.gov}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Foust |first=Jeff |date=18 April 2019 |title=Independent report concludes 2033 human Mars mission is not feasible |url=https://spacenews.com/independent-report-concludes-2033-human-mars-mission-is-not-feasible/ |access-date=29 March 2022 |website=Space News |archive-date=5 May 2026 |archive-url=https://megalodon.jp/2026-0505-0835-23/https://spacenews.com:443/independent-report-concludes-2033-human-mars-mission-is-not-feasible/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In addition, in 2021, China was planning to send a crewed Mars mission in 2033.<ref>{{cite web |date=23 June 2021 |title=China plans its first crewed mission to Mars in 2033 |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/china-plans-its-first-crewed-mission-mars-2033-2021-06-24/ |access-date=20 December 2021 |publisher=Reuters |archive-date=21 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211221031133/https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/china-plans-its-first-crewed-mission-mars-2033-2021-06-24/ |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Private spaceflight|Privately held companies]] such as [[SpaceX]] have also [[SpaceX Mars Colonization Program|proposed plans to send humans to Mars]], with the eventual goal to [[Colonization of Mars|settle on the planet]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Musk |first=Elon |date=1 March 2018 |title=Making Life Multi-Planetary |url=https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/space.2018.29013.emu |journal=New Space |volume=6 |issue=1 |pages=2–11 |bibcode=2018NewSp...6....2M |doi=10.1089/space.2018.29013.emu |issn=2168-0256 |access-date=27 August 2022 |archive-date=29 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190629143329/https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/space.2018.29013.emu |url-status=live}}</ref> As of 2024, SpaceX has proceeded with the development of the [[SpaceX Starship|Starship]] launch vehicle with the goal of Mars colonization. In plans shared with the company in April 2024, Elon Musk envisions the beginning of a Mars colony within the next twenty years. This would be enabled by the planned [[mass manufacturing]] of Starship and initially sustained by resupply from Earth, and [[in situ resource utilization]] on Mars, until the Mars colony reaches full self sustainability.<ref>{{Cite web |author1=Howell |first=Elizabeth |date=13 April 2024 |title=SpaceX's giant Starship will be 500 feet tall for Mars missions, Elon Musk says (video) |url=https://www.space.com/spacex-starship-500-feet-tall-mars-missions-elon-musk |access-date=20 April 2024 |website=Space.com |language=en}}</ref> Any future human mission to Mars will likely take place within [[Exploration of Mars#Launch windows|the optimal Mars launch window]], which occurs every 26 months. The moon Phobos has been proposed as an anchor point for a [[space elevator]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Weinstein |first=Leonard M. |date=2003 |title=Space Colonization Using Space-Elevators from Phobos |url=https://space.nss.org/wp-content/uploads/2003-Space-Colonization-Using-Space-Elevators-From-Phobos.pdf |journal=AIP Conference Proceedings |location=Albuquerque, New Mexico (USA) |publisher=AIP |volume=654 |pages=1227–1235 |bibcode=2003AIPC..654.1227W |doi=10.1063/1.1541423 |s2cid=1661518 |hdl=2060/20030065879 |access-date=6 December 2022 |archive-date=19 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119073347/https://space.nss.org/wp-content/uploads/2003-Space-Colonization-Using-Space-Elevators-From-Phobos.pdf |url-status=live}}</ref> Besides national space agencies and space companies, groups such as the [[Mars Society]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bichell |first=Rae Ellen |date=6 July 2017 |title=To Prepare For Mars Settlement, Simulated Missions Explore Utah's Desert |url=https://www.npr.org/2017/07/06/522060920/to-prepare-for-mars-settlement-simulated-missions-explore-utahs-desert |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221231181009/https://www.npr.org/2017/07/06/522060920/to-prepare-for-mars-settlement-simulated-missions-explore-utahs-desert |archive-date=31 December 2022 |access-date=31 December 2022 |work=[[NPR]] |language=en}}</ref> and [[The Planetary Society]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Boyle |first=Alan |date=29 September 2015 |title=Destination Phobos: 'Humans Orbiting Mars' report goes public |url=https://www.geekwire.com/2015/destination-phobos-humans-orbiting-mars-report-goes-public/ |website=GeekWire}}</ref> advocate for human missions to Mars.

== In culture ==
{{Main|Mars in culture|Mars in fiction}}

{{see also|Planets in astrology#Mars}}
[[File:The War of the Worlds by Henrique Alvim Corrêa, original graphic 15.jpg|thumb|''[[The War of the Worlds]]'' by [[H. G. Wells]], 1897, depicts an [[Alien invasion|invasion of Earth]] by fictional Martians.]]
Mars is named after the [[Ancient Rome|Roman]] [[Mars (mythology)|god of war]] ([[Greek mythology|Greek]] [[Ares]]), but was also associated with the demi-god [[Heracles]] (Roman [[Hercules]]) by ancient Greek astronomers, as detailed by [[Aristotle]].<ref name="DRGA Planetae">[https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aalphabetic+letter%3DP%3Aentry+group%3D4%3Aentry%3Dplanetae-cn "Planetae"], in ''Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities'', pp. 922, 923.</ref> This association between Mars and war dates back at least to [[Babylonian astronomy]], in which the planet was named for the god [[Nergal]], deity of war and destruction.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Koch |first=Ulla Susanne |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8QiwAqGlmAQC |title=Mesopotamian Astrology: An Introduction to Babylonian and Assyrian Celestial Divination |date=1995 |publisher=Museum Tusculanum Press |isbn=978-87-7289-287-0 |pages=128–129 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Cecilia |first=Ludovica |date=6 November 2019 |title=A Late Composition Dedicated to Nergal |url=https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/aofo-2019-0014/html |journal=Altorientalische Forschungen |volume=46 |issue=2 |pages=204–213 |doi=10.1515/aofo-2019-0014 |hdl=1871.1/f23ff882-1539-4906-bc08-049906f8d505 |s2cid=208269607 |issn=2196-6761 |hdl-access=free |access-date=22 March 2022 |archive-date=22 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322014922/https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/aofo-2019-0014/html |url-status=live}}</ref> It persisted into modern times, as exemplified by [[Gustav Holst]]'s orchestral suite ''[[The Planets]]'', whose famous first movement labels Mars "The Bringer of War".<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Reid |first=James |date=2011 |title=An Astronomer's Guide to Holst's ''The Planets'' |url=https://skyandtelescope.org/wp-content/uploads/Reid_on_Holst.pdf |journal=Sky and Telescope |volume=121 |issue=1 |pages=66 |bibcode=2011S&T...121a..66R |access-date=22 March 2022 |archive-date=22 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322014920/https://skyandtelescope.org/wp-content/uploads/Reid_on_Holst.pdf |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Planet symbols|The planet's symbol]], a circle with a spear pointing out to the upper right, is also used as a symbol for the male gender.<ref>{{cite web |title=Solar System Symbols |url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/resources/680/solar-system-symbols |access-date=7 December 2021 |website=NASA Solar System Exploration |date=30 January 2018 |archive-date=20 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211220171351/https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/resources/680/solar-system-symbols/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The symbol dates from at least the 11th century, though a possible predecessor has been found in the Greek [[Oxyrhynchus Papyri]].<ref name="jones-1999">
{{cite book |title=Astronomical papyri from Oxyrhynchus |last=Jones |first=Alexander |date=1999 |pages=62–63 |publisher=American Philosophical Society |isbn=978-0-87169-233-7 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8MokzymQ43IC}}</ref>

[[File:Holst - The Planets - Mars, The Bringer of War (TMO Orchestra, Incheon, 2023).opus|thumb|The first movement of Gustav Holst's The Planets - Mars, The Bringer of War (TMO Orchestra, Incheon, 2023)]]

The idea that Mars was populated by intelligent [[Martian]]s became widespread in the late 19th century. [[Giovanni Schiaparelli|Schiaparelli's]] "canali" observations combined with [[Percival Lowell]]'s books on the subject put forward the standard notion of a planet that was a drying, cooling, dying world with ancient civilizations constructing irrigation works.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Eschner |first1=Kat |title=The Bizarre Beliefs of Astronomer Percival Lowell |url=https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/bizarre-beliefs-astronomer-percival-lowell-180962432/ |access-date=25 December 2021 |website=Smithsonian Magazine |language=en |archive-date=25 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211225111323/https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/bizarre-beliefs-astronomer-percival-lowell-180962432/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Many other observations and proclamations by notable personalities added to what has been termed "Mars Fever".<ref name=fergus04/> In the present day, high-resolution mapping of the surface of Mars has revealed no artifacts of habitation, but pseudoscientific speculation about intelligent life on Mars still continues. Reminiscent of the ''canali'' observations, these speculations are based on small scale features perceived in the spacecraft images, such as "pyramids" and the "[[Cydonia (Mars)|Face on Mars]]".<ref>{{Cite book |last=Plait |first=Philip C. |title=Bad Astronomy: Misconceptions and Misuses Revealed, from Astrology to the Moon Landing 'Hoax' |title-link=Bad Astronomy |date=2002 |publisher=Wiley |isbn=0-471-40976-6 |location=New York |pages=233–234 |oclc=48885221 |author-link=Phil Plait}}</ref> In his book ''[[Cosmos (Sagan book)|Cosmos]]'', planetary astronomer [[Carl Sagan]] wrote: "Mars has become a kind of mythic arena onto which we have projected our Earthly hopes and fears."<ref name=sagan80/>

The depiction of Mars in fiction has been stimulated by its dramatic red color and by nineteenth-century scientific speculations that its surface conditions might support not just life but intelligent life.<ref name=lightman97/> This gave way to many [[science fiction]] stories involving these concepts, such as [[H. G. Wells]]'s ''[[The War of the Worlds]]'', in which Martians seek to escape their dying planet by invading Earth; [[Ray Bradbury]]'s ''[[The Martian Chronicles]]'', in which human explorers accidentally destroy a Martian civilization; as well as [[Edgar Rice Burroughs]]'s series ''[[Barsoom]]'', [[C. S. Lewis]]'s novel ''[[Out of the Silent Planet]]'' (1938),<ref name="sanford09" /> and a number of [[Robert A. Heinlein]] stories before the mid-sixties.<ref name="buker02" /> Since then, depictions of Martians have also extended to animation. A comic figure of an intelligent Martian, [[Marvin the Martian]], appeared in ''[[Haredevil Hare]]'' (1948) as a character in the [[Looney Tunes]] animated cartoons of [[Warner Brothers]], and has continued as part of popular culture to the present.<ref name="rabkin05" /> After the [[Mariner program|''Mariner'']] and [[Viking program|''Viking'']] spacecraft had returned pictures of Mars as a lifeless and canal-less world, these ideas about Mars were abandoned; for many science-fiction authors, the new discoveries initially seemed like a constraint, but eventually the post-''Viking'' knowledge of Mars became itself a source of inspiration for works like [[Kim Stanley Robinson]]'s [[Mars trilogy|''Mars'' trilogy]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Crossley |first=Robert |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=v3TDEDfEPdEC |title=Imagining Mars: A Literary History |date=3 January 2011 |publisher=Wesleyan University Press |isbn=978-0-8195-7105-2 |pages=xiii-xiv |language=en}}</ref>

